# `S-3b` (decision 16): the promo page, served under a strict Content-Security-Policy (nginx's
# `location = /promo`: this origin's scripts and styles only). The first test is tflw `M228-01`'s
# case — an accessibility scan still works on a page that forbids injected scripts. The second is
# `mask` earning its keep: the page carries a clock that ticks every second, so the unmasked page
# never matches its baseline, and the masked page always does. What is captured is the fixed-size
# board the clock sits on, never text a font could reflow: the first baselines were the whole
# section, and CI's Ubuntu wrapped it 32 px shorter than the box's Fedora under one platform key. Baselines were seeded once with
# `tflw run --update-snapshots --only "<this test's name>"` on the box and are committed under
# `tests/snapshots/`; an ordinary run only ever reads and compares.

@ui @catalogOps
test "the promo page, under a strict CSP, still passes an accessibility scan"
  open "/promo"
  expect text "Offers this week" is visible
  expect page has no a11y violations

@ui @catalogOps
test "the promo clock is masked out of the page's snapshot, and without the mask the page never matches"
  open "/promo"
  expect css "[data-clock]" is visible
  expect css "[data-promo-board]" matches snapshot "promo-masked" mask css "[data-clock]"
  expect css "[data-promo-board]" not matches snapshot "promo-unmasked"

@ui @catalogOps
test "the offers subscription is a checkbox the reader can take back"
  open "/promo"
  scroll to css "[data-subscribed]"
  tick field "Email me next week’s offers"
  expect text "You will hear about next week’s offers." is visible
  untick field "Email me next week’s offers"
  expect text "You are not subscribed." is visible
  screenshot "promo-unsubscribed"
